It looks like it's a related issue, When you plot without coord_polar(), one can see the edges of the p1 (180/-180) dataset beyond that of the tiles.
Sorry about the size...

On Mon, Dec 10, 2012 at 7:20 PM, Brandon Hurr <brando...@gmail.com> wrote:
> I can't recommend trying that plot on a MBA :/
>
> /still waiting
>
>
> On Mon, Dec 10, 2012 at 7:13 PM, Mike Lawrence <mike....@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Great! This solves the issue of the contour lines failing to connect.
> >
> > However, having added tiling since my original plot, I now see a second (possibly related?) issue: when you add "+geom_tile(mapping=aes(colour=value,fill=value))", the tiling fails to connect at the seam at the top of the plot. Thoughts on solving this too?
> >
> >
> >
> > On Monday, December 10, 2012 1:43:42 PM UTC-4, winston wrote:
> > > I think the problem is that in your data, theta goes from -180 to 178. So when you plot it, the ends don't quite join up:
> > > ggplot(data = preds, mapping = aes(x = theta, y = phi, z = value)) +
> > >     coord_polar() +
> > >     geom_contour() +
> > >     scale_x_continuous(breaks=seq(-180,180,by=45))
> > >
> > >
> > > If you set the limits from -180 to 180, then it looks like they would join up, but there's a gap between 178 and 180 (or -180):
> > >
> > > ggplot(data = preds, mapping = aes(x = theta, y = phi, z = value)) +
> > >     coord_polar() +
> > >     geom_contour() +
> > >     scale_x_continuous(limits=c(-180,180), breaks=seq(-180,180,by=45))
> > >
> > >
> > > If you add entries for theta=180 that are the same as theta=-180, then it looks like they join up:
> > >
> > > p180 <- subset(preds, theta == -180)
> > > p180$theta <- 180
> > > p1 <- rbind(preds, p180)
> > >
> > > ggplot(data = p1, mapping = aes(x = theta, y = phi, z = value)) +
> > >     coord_polar() +
> > >     geom_contour() +
> > >     scale_x_continuous(limits=c(-180,180), breaks=seq(-180,180,by=45))
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > > -Winston
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > > On Sun, Dec 9, 2012 at 8:16 PM, Mike Lawrence <mike....@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > I don't think it's an issue with the data. Attached are two plots, both including the contours, tiles, and (critically) vlines and gridlines at each 45 degree integer multiple  starting at zero. One plot, "no180.png" emulates the previous plot in that the data have values for theta that are formed by seq(-180,178,by=2), while the second plot, "yes180.png" uses values for theta that are formed by seq(-180,180,by=2). In neither plot do the contour lines connect across the side of the plot opposite theta==0. In the "no180.png" plot, the vlines and gridlines are slightly off being perfectly parallel/perpendicular with the plot margins, as if they've been rotated clockwise a bit. In the "yes180.png" plot, the vlines and gridlines are fine except that there are two lines at the top, to each side of where one would expect the -180/180 line to appear.
